 ## Subtopics
 
-### NeRF Ensemble with Conjoint Pruning
-Uses ensemble averaging and pruning to improve robustness, differing from Aug-NeRF's adversarial perturbation approach. [Link](12608-nerf-ensemble-with-conjoint-pruning.md)
-
-### NeRF in the Wild
-Focuses on handling uncalibrated images and real-world noise, whereas Aug-NeRF assumes calibrated poses but adds robustness via perturbations. [Link](13541-nerf-in-the-wild.md)
-
-### ContraNeRF
-Uses contrastive learning to improve generalization, distinct from Aug-NeRF's explicit worst-case perturbation regularization. [Link](13659-contranerf.md)
-
-### RoGUENeRF
-Focuses on robustness to geometric errors and occlusions, using a different mechanism than triple-level adversarial training. [Link](13731-roguenerf.md)
+### Occluder simulation augmentation
+A data augmentation technique that simulates transient occluders by overlaying randomly positioned and colored squares on training images to test model robustness to in-the-wild occlusions [S2]. Each square consists of ten vertical, colored stripes with colors chosen at random, and they do not have a consistent 3D location across images [S2]. On the Lego dataset, this variation reduces NeRF's PSNR by 14 dB on average, as NeRF represents occluders as colored fog in 3D space, obscuring underlying geometry [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The method adds significant computational overhead due to the inner maximization loop (PGD) for each training step. The number of PGD steps varies by level (3 for coordinates, 1 for features, 1 for output) [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing gradients for the perturbations in addition to the network parameters. The source assumes a batch size of 1 for simplicity, but memory scales with batch size and the number of samples $K$ [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Aug-NeRF reduces average error by 14.3%–26.0% on the LLFF dataset and 12.5%–44.7% on the 360° NeRF-Synthetic dataset compared to baseline NeRF [S1]. It achieves up to 1.5dB PSNR gain in novel view synthesis [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Improves geometry smoothness and generalization to unseen views; robust to noisy supervision images (Gaussian and Shot noise) [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of PGD parameters; excessively strong perturbations (e.g., PGD-10) can deteriorate performance [S1].
-*   **Hardware:** Evaluated on NVIDIA RTX A6000 GPU with 48 GB memory [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The method adds significant overhead due to the inner-loop PGD optimization for perturbation search at each training step. The number of PGD steps varies by level (3 for coordinates, 1 for features, 1 for output) [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing gradients for perturbations in addition to standard NeRF parameters. The feature perturbation $\delta_f$ has dimension $D$ (hidden dimension of the MLP) [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Achieves up to 1.5 dB PSNR gain over baseline NeRF [S1]. On LLFF, it reduces average error by 14.3%–26.0%; on 360° NeRF-Synthetic, by 12.5%–44.7% [S1]. Specific PSNR improvements on LLFF include fern (+1.34), orchids (+1.24), trex (+1.37) [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Implicitly regularizes the loss landscape without sacrificing representation flexibility, unlike explicit regularizers (Laplacian, TV) which can degrade performance [S1]. Robust to noisy supervision (Gaussian and Shot noise), reducing average error by 6.5%–26.9% [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Excessively strong perturbations (e.g., PGD-10) deteriorate training performance [S1]. The method assumes a batch size of 1 for simplicity in the base description [S1].
+*   **Hardware:** Tested on NVIDIA RTX A6000 GPU with 48 GB memory [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Aug-NeRF was proposed by Tianlong Chen, Peihao Wang, Zhiwen Fan, and Zhangyang Wang from The University of Texas at Austin [S1]. It was published in 2022 [S1]. The method is motivated by the observation that NeRF often yields inconsistent and non-smooth geometries when interpolating novel views, a generalization gap that explicit regularizers like Laplacian or Total Variation fail to address without sacrificing representation flexibility [S1]. It draws inspiration from robust data augmentations in convolutional neural networks [S1].
+Aug-NeRF was proposed by Tianlong Chen, Peihao Wang, Zhiwen Fan, and Zhangyang Wang from The University of Texas at Austin [S1]. It was published in 2022 (arXiv:2207.01164v1) [S1]. The work addresses the generalization gap in NeRF where interpolated novel views yield inconsistent and non-smooth geometries [S1]. It draws inspiration from robust data augmentations in convolutional neural networks, applying worst-case perturbations to the NeRF pipeline for the first time [S1].
 
